# Biomimetic silk-spinning duct — a composition, not a named geometry.
#
# This is the example for a request that matches none of the recipes: someone
# describes a natural system, and the chip is the sequence of operations that
# system performs on the fluid, laid end to end.
#
# --- the verbs, and what each becomes ---------------------------------------
#
#   store protein at high concentration   →  `ampulla`, a 4 mm cylinder chamber
#   merge with an exchange buffer         →  Y-junction with curved `via` arms
#   hold them together for a long time    →  a folded run, 4 reversals
#   narrow steadily throughout            →  ONE taper across the whole chain
#   deliver                               →  outlet
#
# Note how the taper is written: markers at the two ENDS of the run and nothing
# in between. Dimension markers loft along a whole chain, so the bare segments
# between them interpolate smoothly. Putting a `taper` on every segment would
# turn the same taper into a staircase of constant-width steps.
#
# The taper cannot cross the merge — a junction of three edges ends a chain —
# so the run is marked from `spin0`, just downstream of the Y, to the outlet.
#
# --- what this chip is NOT --------------------------------------------------
#
# Say all of this to anyone you hand it to:
#
#   * The buffer runs ALONGSIDE the protein, not around it. A Y-merge puts two
#     streams side by side in one rectangular channel; it does not sheathe one
#     in the other the way a natural duct does. Exchange happens by diffusion
#     across a flat interface, which is slower and less uniform.
#   * Nothing here lowers pH or swaps ions by itself. The chip provides contact,
#     time and shear. Whether the protein assembles is chemistry, and it depends
#     on the buffer you actually pump.
#   * No fibre forms inside the chip if all goes well — a solid forming in a
#     0.4 mm channel is a blockage. Expect assembly at or after the outlet, into
#     a coagulation bath.
#   * The narrowing is in the channel width and height only. There is no
#     hyperbolic draw-down, and the elongational strain rate is nothing like a
#     real spinneret's.
#   * OpenChip's fluid estimates are Newtonian. Spidroin dope is strongly
#     shear-thinning, so every pressure figure here is an over-estimate at the
#     narrow end — which is exactly where it matters most.
#
# Expected report: 0 risk · 0 caution · 2 info — the luer-lock thread note, and
# the taper itself, reported because it is a 2.5x change (see below).
# The lock fitting is deliberate: the dope is thousands of times more viscous
# than water and the protein line carries real backpressure, so a slip fit is
# the wrong choice however much tidier its report would look.

chip "Biomimetic silk-spinning duct"
printer standard-4k
size 55 x 88
base 4
wall 2
edge-spacing 3
split two-piece at 2
channel 0.8 x 0.5 rect

fluid silk-dope name "Recombinant spidroin dope" density 1050 viscosity 5 k 0.5 cp 3500 temp 20
fluid exchange-buffer name "Acidic exchange buffer" density 1010 viscosity 0.0015 k 0.59 cp 4100 temp 20

port proteinIn at 16,79 luer-lock  inlet silk-dope 5
port bufferIn  at 39,79 luer-slip  inlet exchange-buffer 20
port outlet    at 27,7  luer-slip  outlet

# Storage: the dope collects here before it enters the duct.
chamber ampulla at 16,70 cylinder 4
path proteinIn -> ampulla

# The merge. Curved arms so the two streams arrive parallel and lie down side
# by side rather than colliding — co-flow is the gentlest contact available,
# and a protein solution is exactly what a head-on collision damages.
node merge at 27,62
path ampulla  -> merge via (16,66) (27,65)
path bufferIn -> merge via (39,66) (27,65)

# The duct: one continuous chain from the merge to the outlet, folding four
# times and narrowing the whole way. 1.0 x 0.6 mm at the top, 0.4 x 0.3 mm at
# the spigot — a 2.5x reduction spread over 210 mm. That does cross the 2x
# taper threshold, so the checker reports it, but at `info` rather than
# `caution` because of how far it is spread: the check weighs the ratio against
# the distance it happens over, and 2.5x across 210 mm is a 0-degree taper.
# Keep it as the report reads; it is the device.
node spin0 at 27,57
path merge -> spin0

path spin0 -> (8,57) taper 1.0x0.6@0 1.0x0.6@1
  -> (8,50) -> (47,50)
  -> (47,43) -> (8,43)
  -> (8,36) -> (47,36)
  -> (47,29) -> (8,29)
  -> (8,22) -> spinEnd(27,22)
# `spinEnd` is NAMED, not written as (27,22) a second time: repeating the
# coordinates would lay down a second node on the same spot with no channel
# between them, and the duct would quietly end here.
path spinEnd -> outlet taper 0.4x0.3@0 0.4x0.3@1
